        <p>We covered our all-time favorites in the <a href="/guides/best-bourbon-whiskey-austin.html">Best Bourbon &amp; Whiskey in Austin</a> guide. This one narrows the lens to whiskey made in Texas, because it's a different animal and because we're a little biased. Below: what the heat does to a barrel, our picks by price, and what to grab when you need a gift that says "I live somewhere interesting."</p>

        <h2>Why Texas Whiskey Tastes Like Texas</h2>
        <p>Kentucky rickhouses swing between cold winters and warm summers. Texas rickhouses swing between warm and blistering, sometimes 40 degrees in a single day. Every time the temperature climbs, whiskey pushes deeper into the wood; every time it drops, it pulls back out, carrying oak sugars, tannin, and color with it. A Texas barrel can do in three or four years what might take a Kentucky barrel eight, and the angels take a much bigger share (evaporation losses of 10 percent a year or more aren't unusual here).</p>
        <p>The result is whiskey that's darker, thicker, and more oak-forward than its age suggests: brown sugar, toffee, leather, dark cherry, and a big drying finish. The trade-off is that Texas whiskey can tip into too much wood if a distiller isn't careful, which is why the best producers here obsess over barrel entry proof, warehouse placement, and blending. Younger doesn't mean thin in Texas. It usually means loud.</p>

        <h2>Everyday Texas Bourbon (Under $50)</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Still Austin "The Musician" Straight Bourbon (Austin)</strong> &mdash; grain-to-glass from 100% Texas grain, distilled a few miles from our East Austin store. Bright and youthful: cornbread, honey, orange peel, a little baking spice. Our default answer to "what's a good local bourbon?"</li>
          <li><strong>Balcones Texas Pot Still Bourbon (Waco)</strong> &mdash; pot-distilled with blue corn in the mash, which gives it a richer, nuttier core than most bourbons at the price. Caramel, roasted corn, and a peppery finish. Great in an Old Fashioned.</li>
          <li><strong>Treaty Oak Red Handed Bourbon (Dripping Springs)</strong> &mdash; a blend of sourced straight bourbons finished and bottled in the Hill Country. Softer and sweeter than Treaty Oak's own-distilled Ghost Hill, and an easy crowd-pleaser.</li>
          <li><strong>Milam &amp; Greene Triple Cask Straight Bourbon (Blanco)</strong> &mdash; a blend of Texas-distilled and Kentucky bourbons married across three kinds of casks. Balanced, vanilla-forward, and one of the smoothest entries into the Texas category.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Step-Up Bottles ($50&ndash;$100)</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Garrison Brothers Small Batch (Hye)</strong> &mdash; the first legal bourbon distillery in Texas, and the bottle most people picture when they hear "Texas bourbon." Thick, sweet, and unapologetic: butterscotch, pecan, cinnamon, and a long oaky finish. A tremendous gift.</li>
          <li><strong>Ironroot Republic Harbinger (Denison)</strong> &mdash; made up on the Red River using techniques borrowed from Cognac, with a heavy dose of heirloom and purple corn. Dense and dark-fruited, with plum, cocoa, and toasted oak. It has picked up serious international hardware and still flies a little under the radar in Austin.</li>
          <li><strong>Treaty Oak Ghost Hill Texas Bourbon (Dripping Springs)</strong> &mdash; Treaty Oak's own-distilled bourbon from Texas corn and wheat. Wheated softness with the Hill Country's big oak on top. Bonus: the distillery is a lovely afternoon out.</li>
          <li><strong>Milam &amp; Greene Port Finished Rye (Blanco)</strong> &mdash; a straight rye finished in port casks. Baking spice up front, red fruit and chocolate at the back. The rye we reach for when someone wants "different, but not weird."</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Texas Single Malts and the Delightfully Weird</h2>
        <p>Not everything worth drinking here is bourbon. Two Texas distilleries make single malts that deserve a spot on any whiskey lover's shelf:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Balcones Texas Single Malt (Waco)</strong> &mdash; the bottle that put Texas whiskey on the world map. Malted barley, Texas heat, and a house style that's all dark honey, dried fig, and toasted grain. Try their <strong>Baby Blue</strong> (blue corn whisky) for something lighter and <strong>Brimstone</strong> if you like smoke that tastes like a campfire, not a peat bog.</li>
          <li><strong>Andalusia Whiskey Co. (Blanco)</strong> &mdash; a small distillery devoted to single malt. <strong>Stryker</strong> is smoked over oak, mesquite, and applewood for a distinctly Texan smokiness; <strong>Revenant Oak</strong> is peated for the Islay-curious; the triple-distilled expression is the gentle entry point.</li>
        </ul>

