        <h2>Texas Whiskey FAQ</h2>
        <h3>What is the best Texas bourbon?</h3>
        <p>Garrison Brothers Small Batch is the most recognized Texas bourbon and a safe answer for most people. For value, Still Austin The Musician and Balcones Texas Pot Still Bourbon are our staff picks under $50, and Ironroot Republic Harbinger is the one whiskey nerds get most excited about.</p>
        <h3>Why is Texas whiskey so expensive?</h3>
        <p>Heat. Texas barrels lose far more whiskey to evaporation than Kentucky barrels, the distilleries here are small, and most use pricier local or heirloom grain. You're paying for less liquid that took more work to make.</p>
        <h3>Is Texas whiskey aged for less time?</h3>
        <p>Usually, yes. Because the Texas climate pulls flavor from the barrel so quickly, many Texas whiskeys are bottled at two to five years and taste older than that. Longer-aged Texas whiskeys exist but tend to be very oak-heavy, which is why blending matters so much here.</p>
